- Raises `ValueError` for various invalid inputs including `'http://eg.com:0'`, `'http://eg.com:-1'`, `'http://eg.com:65536'`, `'http://eg.com:3.14'`, `'http://eg.com:BAD'`, `'http://eg.com:NaN'`, `'http://eg.com: '`
- Raises `ValueError` for `'http://:8080'` (no host)
- Handles IPv6 addresses
- Normalizes scheme to lowercase
- Removes default ports (80 for http, 443 for https)
- Handles host override parameter

The current implementation has duplicate function definitions (the last one wins in Python). The last definition looks correct but let me check the port validation - `parsed.port` in Python's urlparse raises `ValueError` for non-integer ports like `'BAD'`, `'NaN'`, `'3.14'`, but for port `0`, `-1`, `65536`, Python's urlparse actually returns those values without raising an error, so we need to validate them manually.

Also, `'http://eg.com: '` - the space in port - `parsed.port` would raise `ValueError` for that.

The issue with port `0` is that `parsed.port` returns `0` which is falsy, so `if port is not None` check is needed (not `if port`).

